    "mathematical_note": "predicted_G(target|source) = G(source) because TV(target) cancels in the G_item ratio. This identity means pairwise prediction errors are simply |G(source) - G(target)|.",
    "conclusion": "Proportional transfer yields predicted_G(target) = G(source) (total variance cancels in the ratio), so prediction errors directly measure inter-benchmark G_item similarity. All 5 benchmarks cluster in a narrow G_item band (0.863\u20130.896, range=0.033, CV=1.4%), producing low absolute errors (overall MAPE=2.0%, LOO MAPE=1.6%). However, LOO rank correlation is strongly negative (rho=-0.90), because averaging the other 4 benchmarks' structures systematically inverts the ranking: the benchmark with the highest actual G is predicted lowest (its complement has the lowest mean), and vice versa. Within-MC MAPE (1.9%) vs cross-format MAPE (1.9%): no clear format-driven difference, suggesting the narrow G_item range is not format-dependent. Key insight: item measurement reliability (G_item \u2248 0.88) is a robust property across these 5 benchmarks, but the STRUCTURE of variance (which components dominate) differs substantially \u2014 the similar G_item values arise from different variance compositions, not from shared structure."
  }
